 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed revisions are pushed over self-hosted WebSockets rather than five-second HTTPS polling or a hosted realtime service. Self-hosted WebSockets deliver fast updates without recurring realtime vendor fees or provider coupling, in exchange for owning connection capacity, replayable revision storage, reliable publication, and browser reconnect/catch-up logic. Five-second polling was rejected for its update lag and repeated read load despite simpler stateless operation; a hosted realtime service was rejected for recurring vendor costs and provider coupling despite lower connection-management overhead. See `docs/decisions/change-delivery.md`.
